    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change adds per-country national-number length validation to the E.164 telephone normalization, all within the existing single owner of that logic \u2014 `E164Telephone`.\n\n**What I changed** (`E164Telephone.java`):\n- Added a data-driven map `NATIONAL_DIGITS = { \"61\" \u2192 9, \"1\" \u2192 10 }` \u2014 the recognised country codes and the exact national-digit count each requires.\n- Added a small, single-responsibility helper `checkNationalLength(digits)` that finds the matching country-code prefix, computes the remaining (national) digit count, and throws `InvalidTelephoneException` (\u2192 400) when it's wrong. Unrecognised codes fall through to the existing generic 8\u201315 digit rule.\n- Wired one call to it after the generic length check in `normalize(...)`, and updated the class Javadoc.\n\nThe two country codes don't share a prefix (`\"1\"` vs `\"61\"`), so map iteration order is irrelevant. No other class needed touching \u2014 `NormalizeOwnerTelephone`, the exception, and its handler already route this to a 400.\n\n**Verification:** the new `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (which posts `\"+61 123\"` \u2014 only 3 national digits \u2014 expecting 400) passes, and the full `mvnw test` run reports `BUILD SUCCESS` with no failures. The existing valid payloads still pass: `uniqueTelephone()` yields `+61` + 9 national digits, and the `+1` NANP seed data has 10.",
